// Crawly Frontier API: policies.
//
// Policies are static: global policies come from config at frontier startup, a run's policy
// is fixed at CreateRun. Changing a global policy is a deploy.
//
// Sources: global policies are loaded from config when a frontier replica starts; a run's
// RunPolicy is given at CreateRun and is immutable. There is no runtime policy change.
//
// Resolution: global policies (matched by queue key) < run policy < run per-queue override.
// Most specific wins, field by field. Unset fields (proto3 optional) inherit.
//
// Limits are enforced at dispatch against per-queue state shared by all runs:
//   - without run limits, a run's tasks are admitted against the queue's limits and the
//     traffic of every run on the queue;
//   - with run limits (RunPolicy.limits or a RunQueueOverride), they REPLACE the queue's
//     limits for that run: the run is admitted against its own allowance and its own
//     traffic only. Its traffic still counts towards the queue's shared state, so other
//     runs back off while it runs and resume at most one interval after it stops;
//   - a queue block (Outcome.block_queue_for, BlockQueue) stops every run, with or without
//     run limits;
//   - adaptive limits (QueueLimits.adaptive) belong to the queue and slow every run on it,
//     with or without run limits: the interval in force is the longer of the run's and
//     the queue's current one.
//
// Fairness is per queue: runs with work on the same queue share its admitted dispatches by
// weight, per (queue, run, pool) backlog. There is no fleet-wide share of worker slots
// between runs. Fairness comes before TaskSpec.priority, which orders tasks inside one
// run's backlog only.

